{"objects":[{"id":"flow","instance":"instance--b96dad5f-c519-5a1f-a718-912ca4a86c02","objects":["attack-action--1","attack-action--2","attack-action--3","attack-action--4","attack-action--5","attack-action--6","attack-action--7","attack-action--8","attack-action--9","attack-action--10","attack-action--11","attack-action--12","attack-action--13","attack-action--14","attack-action--15","attack-action--16","attack-condition--1","attack-condition--2","attack-condition--3","attack-operator--1","attack-asset--1","instance--763adce8-107c-58c1-9b65-18fefd501b17","file--1","instance--7797ff9e-6685-5e18-adfb-c4d44716e06f","instance--33d457be-25c6-5663-82ef-1321ce9b6639","instance--183f0ea4-2466-5bd9-b5e6-654dbd28e66d","instance--46e1f5ca-0ea1-5df1-b1a9-3318b621fdc4","software--2","instance--436d8a46-af60-5ff1-bcea-35c381dc9970","network-traffic--1","domain-name--1","network-traffic--2","instance--3536a568-610f-5570-bba9-6dfe97bc9c2e","instance--384798bd-a84b-5feb-8d38-7f9d0a087f11","instance--ed6039fe-678b-5ae9-9576-73e6ea35b0c8","instance--7e8c9301-c3f8-55e9-8894-e81f1b6203d9","instance--b6d82c28-0a68-5c6f-b058-c1e072f51852","instance--3622ace1-abf1-5f6e-a5de-a6f584dc1f66","instance--56b40d44-cc57-5e8b-b320-779a685089fd","instance--ede14dbd-ab11-5fb3-876c-d110ff3b2c0b","instance--3bb881aa-a5ab-51ba-b53d-89d26964ce7d","instance--b385e0ce-1ac4-5022-bd7b-3d3ca8cbdf54","instance--57bd700a-faa2-5f72-95f2-2694bcea17c9","instance--105f1e5f-269c-5172-b01b-c94f6fd693b4","instance--3b404379-7d7e-5278-b499-6ac12ee4e90f","instance--88359358-33df-548b-a4cc-a0354f4736ab","instance--3e0f78a3-bb54-55bd-8490-3453d5e06d04","instance--a37796a0-93b2-51bb-be4b-2a9a9e7e35e1","instance--620b376b-602a-5618-88c8-306dd8fd875d","instance--16ec439e-c0fa-55c2-92d7-f04e66533272","instance--82fdbfd2-ff49-532c-9c0a-a6f286cfae0e","instance--b452ed6d-67b8-5808-a358-5200d217f6b4","instance--03922ef2-6745-5331-b3dc-ae0d02c25db6","instance--e5e1f9aa-9fe9-5106-95bc-0f29bb985397","instance--5c474795-7a46-5c6c-af4d-203928d704a3","instance--1fa3126a-bb58-563e-a616-464bda46b52f","instance--d3cd91c5-fb45-5a59-b4e0-4bb5609961ab","instance--b98b21c5-4e8c-51e8-af90-665bb9d3abcd","instance--149e66dd-f433-5e8f-bb4e-748cec2238ff","instance--1c1ce8ac-462f-5f6f-9fcb-fba4d288cb04","instance--bceaa548-f6e9-521c-9fa1-baa6f4f8bd40","instance--be3c9765-554f-5db1-9728-3dc15464f1d5","instance--25739092-0961-53c5-9c60-c662ed47aa57","instance--7a368698-bd73-5b8e-882f-977edf2d0a27"],"properties":[["name","Linux/Ebury credential theft and backdoor operations"],["description","In this blog post, we provide an in-depth analysis of Linux/Ebury - the most sophisticated Linux backdoor ever seen by our researchers. It is built to steal OpenSSH credentials and maintain access to a compromised server."],["scope","malware"],["author",[["name","ESET Research"]]],["external_references",[["item-1",[["source_name","www.welivesecurity.com"],["url","https://www.welivesecurity.com/2014/02/21/an-in-depth-analysis-of-linuxebury/"]]]]]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--ad592d05-6969-5b51-98a8-1ad9b6aab896","120":"instance--5850a8f4-8241-5e73-9bf0-dcd376cd5025","150":"instance--0da1498f-3beb-5916-a249-e0410a4a81e4","180":"instance--d3632784-7391-5a29-83e2-edab51db56c6","210":"instance--8bc47ca5-ca73-5a04-a9ea-918608f10871","240":"instance--6546eef6-c5ad-516e-a417-89916e651648","270":"instance--244e5a69-f465-564e-9d2c-93d4da44fc57","30":"instance--6a1fca3c-fdb6-536a-bbea-61ac2349a700","300":"instance--043a5b44-2864-5565-abce-1909204e5485","330":"instance--e10eb918-2b0b-56f4-9c82-f345ce21276c","60":"instance--33a99d1b-dad8-51f6-be74-e1614db9151b","90":"instance--380c79e9-b1eb-547c-bf01-a011db1201c1"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--1","properties":[["name","Trojanize OpenSSH binaries"],["description","We believe that before using the external library to hook into processes, the author of Linux/Ebury used a patch to modify the source code of OpenSSH, thereby adding \u201cnew functionalities\u201d to the software. The first variants found were modified binaries left on the disk. The three affected files are , and . We have also seen usage of the commands to remove signature from the original packages ( ) and modify the RPM database to update the file hashes with those of the malicious files."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0040","technique":"T1601"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--4f4d03a8-4502-5010-997b-9943e254395b","120":"instance--cefe62ac-5b6e-5ead-84fc-b4c632913a73","150":"instance--4d07a740-2467-5b95-ac8d-4a2ee8edd11e","180":"instance--1205f03c-a457-5a0b-87ef-3bbb7aac110f","210":"instance--96e7dc6a-9f86-5f69-8d45-a566303cd4c0","240":"instance--66237af6-bb19-5a51-9fa2-cbc4a96186f5","270":"instance--836dfd42-b475-50ca-9acf-4f11bd32b89f","30":"instance--efe92997-cc5c-538a-90bc-c09ffb1379f4","300":"instance--9c4a24ae-1426-5cb5-9d7c-792e6dd325b7","330":"instance--06c6a74a-ca7d-5703-88ea-a06a0933d21a","60":"instance--5e4f84e5-56b7-5b8f-9bc3-b4d59d371123","90":"instance--019deb6a-bab3-57db-9a53-11e3564bf7a4"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--2","properties":[["name","Modify shared library"],["description","Later variants of Linux/Ebury do not modify the files directly. Instead, a shared library that is loaded by all OpenSSH executable files is modified to change the behaviour of the programs. The changes are the same as the patched OpenSSH variants, except that the some functions are hooked and patches to the original code are applied at run time. The shared library that is modified on the system is ."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1574.006"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0ce35aea-35a2-52ae-a67b-985306bdefe7","120":"instance--312639d5-e6d9-57b1-850d-28f0a1eac2f6","150":"instance--9551a25e-2558-512e-9894-dd64a8d662bf","180":"instance--403becce-e0ad-5dcd-bc14-7c00453907da","210":"instance--2305f501-2712-5ec6-9d4e-1371b011e937","240":"instance--cf1b8cf6-1cbd-5afb-b653-aa693889dab5","270":"instance--1d3dc59a-40a0-5670-aa3e-1304ff762003","30":"instance--77063d4f-503a-5e9b-b622-15aa2cc6a8e7","300":"instance--beaab67f-8a54-5982-8c5d-9d2ed2d42c9f","330":"instance--4d3274ff-ef14-5a53-99be-01575194a98e","60":"instance--4154a6bf-290b-5fce-a557-5dc0f3e700fb","90":"instance--2992bc0b-df57-54e5-a17b-03a964338c09"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--3","properties":[["name","Hook functions on library load"],["description","To enable the different features of the malware, a was added in the . This function is called when the library is loaded. This function detects which binary it was loaded from, applies patches to the original code, and hooks functions from the original import table."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1574.006"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0f6e4da7-a5f4-54d7-866f-3bc6e8bcb019","120":"instance--015254b5-0906-577e-b3e3-3899f837d118","150":"instance--f7af5b8c-330a-58d5-b0da-a3d5f0bc6ad0","180":"instance--9f27ab21-2382-567d-b022-74ad5a29bd08","210":"instance--d799eb0a-7600-5b1a-a298-c47a78ceae4b","240":"instance--911c2c52-5974-5e08-b0b6-a9084d57c6ee","270":"instance--286444b4-97a1-58cd-9ca3-cffa04a15351","30":"instance--92192c38-5143-50e0-8c39-a96ea86e32e2","300":"instance--82bb3de0-bea5-5651-a1de-583283258c6d","330":"instance--4f0945c8-f644-50ce-8796-fea52a29dda1","60":"instance--16c43a2d-7356-521d-a507-5922e2c3337b","90":"instance--abce5c62-339a-5ed5-a0b8-dd66136cfe2f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--4","properties":[["name","Resolve executable address space"],["description","After unpacking, the malware loads various functions it requires using multiple calls to the function. Linux/Ebury then discovers the original executable address space by calling ( ) and passing the returned handle to ...). This will work even if is enabled on a system. This behaviour gives Linux/Ebury the ability to walk the import table of an ELF executable and replace the original imported function address in memory."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1620"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--a469636c-0cb2-50b9-92f9-644c8aa98197","120":"instance--d57b3a21-e910-5879-8148-40165cea451a","150":"instance--58250ef4-5bb4-5bf3-b33e-89493bbb395d","180":"instance--3e769cf2-c1e5-5d2e-81d4-892f48ce4d86","210":"instance--f6499e41-749c-5d7f-9346-96c08d0d5dfd","240":"instance--d9dcdfc8-5f42-546a-803a-0efcd5d4cf91","270":"instance--c9afd0f6-97ce-57b2-9d60-3557b6214809","30":"instance--75f82155-f5ff-5619-8bf8-aae22937cab0","300":"instance--25e2d26e-1b35-56bd-b9aa-096c2d3b0e3c","330":"instance--e0483688-da10-5376-8cf2-050bcc3b70f0","60":"instance--1293d9cc-16b2-5c67-a453-bd144aab133a","90":"instance--2b43cd52-10db-5b15-8ad5-c4b59496528b"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--5","properties":[["name","Suppress logging"],["description","The logging functions are hooked so that whenever the backdoor is used, nothing gets sent to the logging facility, leaving no trace of the backdoor in the log files on disk. If the backdoor is not in use, logging will behave normally and function calls will get redirected to the original function implementation."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1562.001"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--54fc1f38-f4e3-523e-b07c-73889b0d8df7","120":"instance--9cf45948-7040-5d7a-a1d5-3cb4db0c1af6","150":"instance--13bb13cc-e02b-51d2-8d7d-40e5613b6e5a","180":"instance--e33bc6ee-771e-54fe-a772-74dfdaad420d","210":"instance--286f51a3-8fc6-55ec-978e-8b05ebda3655","240":"instance--14659696-be77-5460-beb1-4b5eac126e43","270":"instance--f48ed1a6-2895-5cd2-9b1c-e30ac2bc8921","30":"instance--7b803e61-16ac-5720-92cd-8f2a456a52b7","300":"instance--76c24361-5143-5c43-8e4d-707c619e6038","330":"instance--f395cfc7-395a-5001-9552-a4fec6dbc53a","60":"instance--760885bb-9294-5a5b-93a2-a410e1ea4112","90":"instance--8e0d1a12-8611-598d-9a7d-40f2525e4781"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--6","properties":[["name","Patch code segments at runtime"],["description","When hooking from the import table is not an option, Linux/Ebury will modify the code segment by patching specific sections of the program to redirect call instruction to its own implementation."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--7cccbcd0-a724-54fb-be4f-ac523d26f7d0","120":"instance--738e44b2-1357-5eb3-82bd-8a0ce8942b72","150":"instance--d31f4735-6339-5929-8d7c-d30863cf166f","180":"instance--2252393d-d801-5931-ad38-4b53374a9880","210":"instance--065b5ad5-023a-5336-b458-e9c9dcda2976","240":"instance--5ea0dd2b-5eac-51f8-855a-4cbcb801bc2d","270":"instance--bba66874-a069-56c6-bb95-967cb3cefc9e","30":"instance--6377c09d-ecb4-5567-a2f4-4dde56ff4f98","300":"instance--ede3430f-b16b-58ae-be71-1d1f1a194336","330":"instance--7772f482-8ee2-569a-b600-7528c065d4e0","60":"instance--98139a98-16b0-54fe-8c77-3473c1b520cd","90":"instance--5cb9cc95-e190-5a68-985d-0a94d39299e6"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--7","properties":[["name","Recover from segmentation faults"],["description","Before attempting to modify the code segment, the program carefully sets a handler to intercept any segmentation fault it may cause. More specifically, the handler will be called if the process receives a signal. In the case such signal is caught, Linux/Ebury will simply abort its task and let the do its legitimate behavior. The way to recover from a segmentation fault is interesting. Before doing something that could potentially crash the process, is called to create a snapshot of the current state. Then, if an access violation ever happens, is used in the signal hander to restore to the previous state."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1027"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--5150f7cc-6f25-5eb3-a6fc-5099ced4caf4","120":"instance--64a408ce-c543-55d6-a9dd-206ce7cadbb2","150":"instance--1b71e413-cfd2-528c-8792-dc7dd1bb9938","180":"instance--aa12ce11-0165-52b6-b72d-e4d9070e9fbb","210":"instance--5d8b0f08-14bc-58f4-9316-b25a92dd09a6","240":"instance--34f96b84-9453-53f2-ae07-d28c04665e6c","270":"instance--415f1d54-1cd4-5ba7-8071-4aa99bc946ac","30":"instance--18834cac-b4c4-57b0-88ea-5a62fdb27cf9","300":"instance--de4f9957-1ac6-5782-86ea-f1a3b6a9ad13","330":"instance--8eff940b-86bb-556f-8c85-c7c47577b3ed","60":"instance--14a123fd-5626-5e69-8257-6dcd64c28ec0","90":"instance--02294ff1-7261-5db8-8e0d-b9e818922053"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--8","properties":[["name","Activate backdoor via SSH version string"],["description","The backdoor is activated by sending specially-crafted data inside of the SSH client protocol version identification string."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1071.001"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--180f2983-9c1a-56f4-99d6-c990125f7baa","120":"instance--c7f65318-7600-57f4-a8ac-992357e695ff","150":"instance--95523e1a-0a1d-52ef-944c-23f9cbc50233","180":"instance--bc8eb48d-411d-5877-aa9b-966476b71207","210":"instance--4cc5026e-07f9-5327-9912-5d278a0a3321","240":"instance--d6a7d51d-39a4-5eb3-8831-f23437781b95","270":"instance--cac30e05-5f01-5b4f-b5c0-6b975297df2d","30":"instance--cb0751b0-c027-5895-a460-a72e48920c71","300":"instance--f41cdc1f-abb7-5ff7-81c2-bf2235a7faab","330":"instance--b114cbda-9ec8-5612-b2f8-93c36811cafb","60":"instance--f28e5296-2041-5fe9-992d-4c70d549191e","90":"instance--3ea39244-da0f-5435-8745-f2afa8b13872"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--9","properties":[["name","Bypass SSH authentication"],["description","Once the backdoor password is verified, the process will allow any password to work during a password authentication. If , or is disabled, it will enable them to make sure it works. It will also disable all logging of the successful session creation. It will be like nothing happened."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1556"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--db5fab04-54d4-5eec-a429-185861eb2719","120":"instance--985391bd-d017-5935-ab31-3b4b56331b02","150":"instance--092bb98d-fc30-5f69-b6b7-e3024f3ec65a","180":"instance--755129e6-612e-5b63-a780-1c886d98909b","210":"instance--431723d9-dd8c-5190-9dab-6f346173c044","240":"instance--5b9fb821-54fe-5f22-bba3-038263245910","270":"instance--3c6777cb-6430-544b-b31a-01b198524553","30":"instance--c070071b-a9a3-52fe-8956-a51744db200b","300":"instance--a9a4289c-d995-5783-9544-a8b45052eb56","330":"instance--344009cc-e024-5c68-9246-51e85e00d45c","60":"instance--86c77621-2c1d-59c3-b398-e62f4b48a3dd","90":"instance--67627b08-1913-5f3d-8318-52d3bda9c1c3"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--10","properties":[["name","Intercept credentials"],["description","Credentials are intercepted at multiple locations, when they are typed or used by the victim:\n\n:  Whenever someone logs in a system infected with Linux/Ebury, the daemon will save the password and send it to the exfiltration server.\n\n: Even if the attempt is unsuccessful, the username and password used will be sent to the operators. They will be formatted differently, however, allowing the operators to differentiate these invalid credentials from the valid ones.\n\n: When someone uses the ssh client on an infected server, Linux/Ebury will intercept the password and sent it to its exfiltration server.\n\n: When the ssh client on an infected server prompts the user for an private key passphrase, the passphrase will be sent to the remote exfiltration server.\n\n: When a private key is used to authenticate to a remote server, the unencrypted version is intercepted by the malware. Unlike passwords, it will not send the key to the exfiltration server. Instead, it will store it memory and wait for the operators to fetch the key with the Xcat command.\n\n: The keys added to an OpenSSH agent are also intercepted by the malware. Both the unencrypted key itself and the passphrase typed by the user will be logged."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0006","technique":"T1556.004"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--6b7baffd-afaf-5472-9af2-a263119a77ef","120":"instance--83f38e63-e29a-53f4-99d9-927a195c453f","150":"instance--3e720145-b7a1-5bd6-8a7d-bb13519675d1","180":"instance--bdce060f-d96c-5b06-b8f4-1ab1527fd01d","210":"instance--a4feb251-e6e3-5680-a712-d59c92f73ccf","240":"instance--9a564ca6-b753-5b06-880c-b3aac25c1e73","270":"instance--76861a89-f006-5e0d-8c5b-ebdcb5e926dc","30":"instance--cf41e64d-4a4b-58d9-9962-72d278b2c489","300":"instance--ac1d6edd-482f-5f13-9348-b605afcc260c","330":"instance--ed78dfa1-f300-564f-b2cd-6c99552b9a68","60":"instance--979accb3-1b3e-5af4-9182-78392cbaf904","90":"instance--ae721c42-320e-5ea4-9c77-339a05b6e12f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--11","properties":[["name","Exfiltrate passwords over DNS"],["description","When a password is intercepted by Linux/Ebury, the information is sent to a remote server via a crafted DNS request sent to a specific IP address. The malware creates a regular A record request that will be sent on UDP port 53. The domain name requested contains encrypted and hexadecimal-encoded data as well as an IP address."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0010","technique":"T1048.003"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--f1e35721-7ac2-54fe-bcf6-5456d2faeaa3","120":"instance--3ff6777e-fcba-5b31-bf0f-3ddf77f6030d","150":"instance--cc6192f9-21a5-5d1a-b954-6a52d989b82d","180":"instance--62a87260-0181-5f49-bc05-733216e4d822","210":"instance--2f2d05c5-8e60-5ac2-b7af-e03027d67957","240":"instance--79c1ec18-d792-5d3d-a7bc-d94fe8b124cf","270":"instance--a035d357-e930-5d33-ab8d-d0314092d036","30":"instance--3af551b8-f6ac-5f54-9f6d-403fecdc69eb","300":"instance--298b5d2a-bf93-51e9-b052-19ce36114a5d","330":"instance--c8ea0da0-a19a-5766-b0d1-8524844a59d1","60":"instance--a204f87d-ace7-5a9e-b3d1-13202bdc5c7b","90":"instance--fff4de11-3f84-52c8-bb77-192210612a62"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--12","properties":[["name","Set exfiltration server with Xver"],["description","There are 2 ways by which Linux/Ebury can choose a server where the DNS packets are sent. First, it can be set explicitly by the operator when sending an Xver command."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1105"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--cac05670-e90e-512c-99cc-c0adf909c3ef","120":"instance--43922820-ddd3-5742-a88d-49c20f8e3fd5","150":"instance--884271a6-8d7b-5c96-9ece-61be4126898b","180":"instance--373a94ef-653d-5c24-96f7-d0cf326d3aea","210":"instance--e58b782a-e75e-5141-bdf0-6d47dbed96bd","240":"instance--5960e46a-3307-5101-b96f-f1bae10e323d","270":"instance--a5ae1125-6ca5-54f1-b9d3-0091a0a38367","30":"instance--e7c521dd-fe09-528b-bb67-1ec53389b876","300":"instance--582e0864-cb5c-5de1-b6c0-e162ae91e8bc","330":"instance--3d5550f3-3e33-5d71-b71e-db2a0a957e5e","60":"instance--b83fc757-0bd4-5b63-a13e-e5b8ef5956d2","90":"instance--2a745819-837b-5585-a366-81a34117a6e5"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--13","properties":[["name","Query generated domain for A and TXT records"],["description","The second method uses an algorithm to generate a domain name dynamically. This domain name will be queried for its A and TXT records. The TXT record will be used to verify that it is under the control of the operators using public key cryptography."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1568.002"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--2accaaf8-d071-542e-9a10-06866a96b394","120":"instance--75ef5b2f-7c71-566e-baf2-29f54ced8138","150":"instance--73017f0a-a5f3-5dca-b91c-a2d37bcfe21b","180":"instance--2f1b6e5f-d3b0-59e5-b3c8-6c1399d1ce2a","210":"instance--c7aedd91-3346-5b75-9e3a-842851881a25","240":"instance--2146989b-d8a2-53f0-8a1c-cd3d976227e9","270":"instance--1b0bbd91-fa2a-5897-96d0-9e5918961c9c","30":"instance--e6b73353-364e-5586-a07b-e1fdc92ad2d3","300":"instance--0fdc5473-d5b9-5fed-91a3-21958da6287b","330":"instance--fd1cd9e0-5761-5725-801f-bf88b65914e8","60":"instance--a58b8357-6abc-58d7-ad2b-bec48fffcd9b","90":"instance--e8daf33f-0240-5544-9f99-3be7ac96fc1d"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--14","properties":[["name","Fetch stored credentials with Xcat"],["description","Three commands are available to the malicious actors to ease the management of the compromised server. A command is appended to the backdoor password before being encrypted. When the backdoor identifies a command, it interprets it instead of starting a shell.  Here is a list of commands that can be processed by the backdoor:\n\n: print all the passwords, passphrases, and keys saved in the shared memory and exit."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0009","technique":"T1005"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--c80ca3ef-db0a-5372-968e-c17eb31a79ca","120":"instance--69989f45-927f-57be-92e5-5102fccd9f11","150":"instance--9b02c549-b183-5406-a100-249fb1868d16","180":"instance--c1d2a890-ed8d-5123-92d5-ceeaa937ee87","210":"instance--b785b7b9-02d5-5d04-be0c-0d02d4f85620","240":"instance--cde2d3fb-a23f-5188-9d7d-6c7f3cd3cebb","270":"instance--3fa59d94-25bb-5c51-af21-1a7c1fd91f66","30":"instance--fdd04191-1a03-5134-b88e-a55240471931","300":"instance--dddeb587-c160-5bdc-a385-f5d294f11e92","330":"instance--a13b6b46-2b8e-5660-b86f-03747a07fe24","60":"instance--d996db22-4d65-536a-83bf-a1db02b37136","90":"instance--258e2cac-1806-5147-8d61-a10d64a86e46"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--15","properties":[["name","Query malware version with Xver"],["description",": print the installed Linux/Ebury version and exit. Xver also accept an optional four (4) byte argument. If present, it will set the exfiltration server IP address to the given one."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0007","technique":"T1082"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--878d252f-de04-5857-ad0a-e8560794a9aa","120":"instance--bf65c103-2305-50be-8098-7718a8b010c5","150":"instance--348f71b8-78ee-5edf-accb-cd536c0db67d","180":"instance--e786d536-88e4-5ad4-8526-ae120c505f45","210":"instance--d3f3806c-3238-5b69-a0ca-334a1453e3dd","240":"instance--6dc42515-8e0a-5d8f-9dcb-cc6f0eb80f24","270":"instance--6a335c70-0c01-578c-9e01-614ace562750","30":"instance--c34e4c14-a710-535c-afd4-17d8ea759e88","300":"instance--60b19ce6-d4d3-5ce5-b2fe-3427fd0ea9a4","330":"instance--dea41a44-5c5a-520f-8975-25088fdd166c","60":"instance--e8f2f47b-1824-589b-af23-ab4291258936","90":"instance--67989025-38e3-56c8-b903-f474fc067511"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--16","properties":[["name","Bind tunnel socket to interface IP"],["description",": takes a four (4) byte argument. When creating a tunnel to a remote host, bind the client socket to the specified interface IP address."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1572"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--35652c79-2c41-5d60-81ba-b5e47aa554bf","120":"instance--53906203-fffb-5af3-b112-44a9b4b49b8f","150":"instance--465fbc8a-7b4c-569f-be00-c0fbc137fcfc","180":"instance--a653c96b-3673-5dfa-968e-188fffe4cc04","210":"instance--6a344cf5-bd5c-52db-84f5-23c7701c7a5e","30":"instance--913561d1-b5f3-54d2-8272-159233dfde54","330":"instance--d59fe52d-c897-5f3a-919a-c2273cb8a8b3","60":"instance--f1fd6a3d-bb83-543d-a379-39d4632978f7","90":"instance--4cd6fce2-0d24-57f0-b161-12a867cd1100","branch:False":"instance--f7e897b3-3607-5ea5-8d15-5f83ba8a60da","branch:True":"instance--39b5ac19-b0dd-5674-a7d9-d75e659fe9ea"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--1","properties":[["description","Once the backdoor password is verified"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--2ff8b105-17d8-5bc8-9736-a71ca9281068","120":"instance--fe9b11f7-9a07-51c8-93b2-02f34148833b","150":"instance--15f0676c-2f2f-58cf-99a3-b5eade2d286e","180":"instance--cdc5e3d9-a0b4-5995-98a3-d1a29532ffb1","210":"instance--75ed5df8-ff2f-5667-9748-b8587a1ee318","30":"instance--46009917-4258-54e5-9e91-c46c4a45d34d","330":"instance--54845373-a5f0-5af0-b348-5b0a3b4fbe67","60":"instance--f1f8d9a5-1f2a-55c4-af9a-f2629bc0827a","90":"instance--ddfb6e54-4af9-5771-910f-b4c7ba6e9860","branch:False":"instance--64607b7d-a4bd-56e7-9116-7bc21201c8bf","branch:True":"instance--f635d174-aa29-531f-b9c8-0cc481c6c1dd"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--2","properties":[["description","When a password is intercepted by Linux/Ebury"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--64fa8274-9976-53b9-811c-a0e7e805b799","120":"instance--ce384754-4013-5515-ac17-4456e1d80bbb","150":"instance--99275651-db0d-57ec-a13c-6e557a4b9c7c","180":"instance--cbaa25ac-4f14-51f0-9962-f76fe36e7cd6","210":"instance--1c4fda5b-4605-5040-a88a-48f1df4144aa","30":"instance--39743932-1858-5655-a859-b27c0902b87a","330":"instance--c116df79-3c10-5931-96ec-25b4174ae13a","60":"instance--75b926aa-d189-5cd6-bc7b-5a947c88de41","90":"instance--4931e63d-9e52-5183-bbd8-7db1814b0f21","branch:False":"instance--fc00a7ae-0a9a-58b7-b24a-5def4ea9feab","branch:True":"instance--e3424864-637f-5b96-b87c-a8dcc22d8b9e"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--3","properties":[["description","since version 1.3.2, Linux/Ebury will not send any information to a remote server if an interface is in promiscuous mode."]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--36aad375-d405-5e3b-9a7d-4346c75c9f45","120":"instance--acc72955-e581-59b3-80b5-35fb047df4b8","150":"instance--d5d0ec7c-da1b-5d39-86e4-8de8792ab05d","180":"instance--047e7862-d9c4-5716-b778-899a86595b48","210":"instance--650a6d3c-5cb0-5f82-83ec-c5d828e2502b","240":"instance--3f08654d-fd5e-59cb-af47-8bbcee29de39","270":"instance--e175549d-38ff-5527-bdb8-de5e1a12f356","30":"instance--c6e60459-b005-50cd-9e68-66ef210b5456","300":"instance--f90b34f0-8620-5a56-9e11-4db2b889aac4","330":"instance--93872aee-cf81-52fa-aa93-f7a84a833dbd","60":"instance--5a877a06-3649-5bba-9461-5faee75ea842","90":"instance--68af6616-0e27-5395-b4ff-5d95b55a2405"},"id":"OR_operator","instance":"attack-operator--1","properties":[["operator","OR"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--253917f6-0fb3-5fc5-bd71-946fe7eb5b45","120":"instance--3f50f35e-a337-5d55-a037-5b923804b373","150":"instance--e1655f18-961e-5a7c-8605-a43fa92fe621","180":"instance--c536c45c-6edd-5cb6-8ee3-046111b98062","210":"instance--42f3c144-a229-5f85-b5be-d7f7668d557c","240":"instance--691fbaf3-af2f-5133-81cc-3f20013aafe4","270":"inst